  "name": "CD 3 - T CELL MARKER",
  "code": "1505",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\"><strong>Note: </strong>The assay result is sensitive to preanalytical variables (sample storage, transport, time to processing). Please review with clinical details (if required).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\"><strong>Instrument:&nbsp;</strong>BD FACS LYRIC AUTOMATED 10 colour Flo.w cytometer using BD FACS SUITE.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\"><strong>Technique:&nbsp;</strong>Flow Cytometry Single Platform Technology, beads based count.</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Specimens are sequentially gated as follows:</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">CD45 Leucocyte common antigen &gt;&gt; CD3 T-cells &gt;&gt; CD4 Helper T-cells</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Methodology: LNW (Lyse no wash).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\"><strong>Interpretation guidelines:</strong></span></p>\n<ol>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">CD4 counts may change up to 30% without any change in clinical status due to biological and method variation. Therefore, trends over time should be monitored rather than change in a specific test result. (Ref: J Wallach: Interpretation of laboratory medicine)&nbsp;</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">For monitoring, both CD4 % &amp; Absolute CD4 counts should be considered. However, Absolute CD4 counts can show variation as it is dependant absolute lymphocyte count (CD45) &amp; total T-cell count (CD3) within a sample which in turn are affected by various infective/inflammatory conditions. While CD4 % is considered a stable parameter as it shows minimal biological variation &amp; least dependent on absolute counts within a sample</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">An unexpected result must be repeated before changing therapy.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Very low CD4/8 absolute cell counts and percentages though satisfactorily acquired by the flow cytometer could be associated with cellular degeneration, hence correlation with clinical findings &amp; viral load is advised. If clinically indicated kindly repeat the test with fresh sample.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Factors affecting CD4/CD8 counts:</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Circadian rhythm, Age, Sex, ethnic origin, stress, effect of drugs, presence of anti-lymphocyte antibody, splenectomise, menstrual cycle, smoking, infections, pregnancy, Time of collection, delay in analysis, sequential use of different assay and sample storage.</span></p>",
